{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790346963,"nonce":"UHZanXvxvuPSyCVP0r1mzg75epM=","message":"Diagnosed (2026-09-25): Cranelift JIT relocation overflow, not a stack fault. cranelift-jit's default SystemMemoryProvider maps each function and data object separately; ~200 nested coroutines each reserve a 64 MiB fiber stack (~12.8 GB of mappings), so on Intel macOS later JIT code lands more than 2 GiB from earlier code and a colocated PC-relative call/data reference (X86CallPCRel4/X86PCRel4) overflows: cranelift-jit compiled_blob.rs:142 panics on i32::try_from, the first-call compile aborts. arm64 uses veneers, hence Intel only. Reproduced under Rosetta (official/cstack.lua exit 134). Two fixes: (a) absolute references for every non-self call (tested, refs/backup/fix-macx86-cstack/absolute-refs = 883dafd6; passes Rosetta and conformance on arm64 and the NUC, but x86 Cranelift binary_trees 9-12% slower, method_call ~4%, and two conservative-GC cases flaky under Rosetta); (b) a JITMemoryProvider reserving one contiguous range per JIT module (codegen unchanged). (b) was not written: the file write was refused. Awaiting the user's choice.","files":null}]}